block eng_w    rust
block lawn     stone lawn ceil
block pond     stone water ceil
block trunk    wood
block leaves   crop

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- dome ---
# First, because a cap is a sphere with the rest cut away and the cut takes
# everything under it: a radius-60 ball whose rim meets the drum's edge at
# y=170 and whose top is twelve voxels above it.
ball ice 80 122 80 60
clear 0 0 0 159 170 159

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ drive ---
# The bell opens downwards from a cold throat: the ship is coasting.
cone steel 80 80 10 0 4 9 shell=1
cone steel 80 80 3 9 3 9
# The tip is solid reactor, the hull skin goes over it, and the shadow shield
# above it, a collar wider than the hull, is what everything else is behind.
cone reactor 80 80 4 10 7 22
cone steel 80 80 7.5 24 7.5 27

# ------------------------------------------------------------ engineering ---
# The lower cone is the other half of the ship: the drive at the bottom, the
# reaction mass round the spine, and six machine decks hung between them. None
# of it has held air since the war - the hatch under D1 is what keeps that out
# of the decks above - so everything down here is suit work, in the dark, with
# the shaft as the only way through. It is reached by opening the lower spine
# hatch, and nothing else.
#
# Every slab is the hull line r(y) = 4 + (y-10)*26/140 read at its own height,
# less the voxel and a half the hull skin takes, so a deck meets the inside of
# the plating rather than pushing out through it.
#
#   y 104  E6  stores       crates lashed down, the widest deck
#   y  90  E5  pump hall    the machine room, walled, off the deck's south side
#   y  76  E4  mass deck    catwalk between the four tanks
#   y  62  E3  mass deck    the same, one deck down
#   y  48  E2  coolant      where the tanks stand on their feet
#   y  34  E1  reactor gallery, right over the shadow shield
cone fl_eng 80 80  7.0  34  7.0  34
cone fl_eng 80 80  9.6  48  9.6  48
cone fl_eng 80 80 12.2  62 12.2  62
cone fl_eng 80 80 14.8  76 14.8  76
cone fl_eng 80 80 17.4  90 17.4  90
cone fl_eng 80 80 20.0 104 20.0 104
# The four tanks of reaction mass, standing on E2 and reaching the hatch under
# D1. They are skins rather than solid: a tank with a wall and an inside is a
# place, and four solid pillars were only ever a way of using up the room. The
# decks they pass through are cut back out of them, so a suit can go down one
# from the top - which is the only way into E3 and E4 that is not the shaft.
cone tank 86.5 80 3 48 3 110 shell=1
cone tank 80 86.5 3 48 3 110 shell=1
cone tank 73.5 80 3 48 3 110 shell=1
cone tank 80 73.5 3 48 3 110 shell=1
cone air 86.5 80 2 49 2 109
cone air 80 86.5 2 49 2 109
cone air 73.5 80 2 49 2 109
cone air 80 73.5 2 49 2 109
# A hatch out of each tank onto the deck it stands on: one voxel of its skin,
# taken out on the side that faces the shaft.
clear 84 49 80 84 49 80
clear 80 49 84 80 49 84
clear 76 49 80 76 49 80
clear 80 49 76 80 49 76

# E5's pump hall: the one room down here with walls of its own, on the south
# side of the deck with its door onto it. Two pump stacks and the pipe between
# them, which is what the tanks were feeding.
